From c5a4336f3a16bd62c575a3ec3c009c19a4452edd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aaron Paterson <apaterson@pm.me>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:47:40 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] virtio_balloon: report free pages in buffers the device reads

Free page reporting hands the device scatterlists of free pages as device-writable buffers, and the device needs only their addresses to release the memory behind them. A device implemented on a transport that gives it the addresses of readable buffers alone, as macOS's Virtualization framework does for a custom Virtio device (VZVirtioQueueElement exposes read buffers as memory and write buffers only as a destination to copy into), cannot act on writable ones. Reporting the pages as readable buffers lets such a device release them; the device returns each buffer when it is done, as before.
---
 drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c b/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
index 1b93d8c..5784f6f 100644
--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
@@ -204,8 +204,13 @@ static int virtballoon_free_page_report(struct page_reporting_dev_info *pr_dev_i
 	struct virtqueue *vq = vb->reporting_vq;
 	unsigned int unused, err;
 
-	/* We should always be able to add these buffers to an empty queue. */
-	err = virtqueue_add_inbuf(vq, sg, nents, vb, GFP_NOWAIT);
+	/*
+	 * We should always be able to add these buffers to an empty queue.
+	 * They go to the device as buffers it reads: the device only needs
+	 * where the free pages are, and a device whose transport hands it the
+	 * addresses of readable buffers alone can then release them.
+	 */
+	err = virtqueue_add_outbuf(vq, sg, nents, vb, GFP_NOWAIT);
 
 	/*
 	 * In the extremely unlikely case that something has occurred and we
